  username and password ([38;5;153mindex.html:9-10[39m), so at the moment [38;5;153mlogin[39m is called there is no user id in hand yet. That means
  "add a parameter" resolves three different ways, and they aren't equally cheap to change later:

  - [1mServer returns it, no new parameter[0m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m gains [38;5;153muserId[39m in its [3mreturn[0m value ([38;5;153m{ success, userId, user }[39m), and
    tracking happens after the response. Buys you the id that actually identifies the account; costs a change to the
    return shape instead of the signature. This is the normal shape for auth, and it's what "track who logged in"
    usually means. Rules out nothing.
  - [1mCaller passes an id it already has[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m. Buys an exact signature match to your
    request; costs the caller needing a source for the id that doesn't exist yet in this app, so I'd have to invent one
    (hidden field, config value). Hard to undo quietly once other callers adopt the argument order.
  - [1mClient-generated correlation id[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, requestId)[39m, a random per-attempt id for tracing a
    request through logs. Buys traceability before authentication succeeds; costs that it tracks the [3mattempt[0m, not the
    person — it won't answer "who logged in."

  My recommendation is the first. "Track who logged in" is a post-authentication fact, and the server is the only thing
  that knows it; threading an id inward before the user is authenticated means logging an id the client asserted rather
  than one that was verified.

  One related note either way: [38;5;153mlogin[39m currently logs the username to the console, and any of these adds a second
  identifier to that log line. In a real app that's a PII-in-logs question — here it's a stub, so I'll flag it rather
  than solve it.
